\section{Description of the data}
\label{sec:EDA}

This dataset contains anonymized records for over 300,000 international graduates. It is sourced from Kaggle but originates from HESA (Higher Education Statistics Agency), the official body responsible for UK higher education data. The analysis here focuses on UK international students' employability outcomes, so the dataset was filtered to students who studied in the UK, giving 74,901 rows. It combines graduate-outcomes survey data with visa, language, and academic information, allowing predictive analysis of two outcomes: Employment Status (employed, unemployed, or continuing education) and Salary (annual salary for those employed).

The data set contains 15 variables: \textbf{11 categorical} (Employment\_Status, Visa\_Type, Country\_of\_Origin, Language\_Proficiency, Field\_of\_Study, Gender, University\_Ranking, Education\_Level, Internship\_Experience, Job\_Sector and Region\_of\_Study) and \textbf{4 numerical} (Age, GPA, Salary, Years\_Since\_Graduation).

Moving to \textbf{exploratory data analysis (EDA)}, this section focuses on the distributions of the two main response variables, Employment Status and Salary, in relation to the other predictors. For Employment Status, we examine its own distribution alongside its relationship with each categorical predictor. For Salary, we examine its own distribution as well as its relationship with both the numerical and categorical predictors. This section focuses specifically on the distributions of Employment Status and Salary, since these shape how the data was prepared and structured for the modeling that follows.

Figure~\ref{fig:employment} shows that the data is imbalanced between the three employment status categories, with 18,637 graduates in Continuing Education, 39,038 Employed and 17,226 Unemployed. Since this analysis focuses on employability, Continuing Education was excluded, as these graduates have not yet entered the labor market. Even after this exclusion, the figure still shows that the remaining data is still imbalanced between the Employed and Unemployed categories. This imbalance motivates the choice of evaluation approach used later in the analysis, the use of precision-recall curves rather than ROC curves , which will be discussed further in the Analysis chapter. 


