\section{Description of the methods}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Classification methods}

 
Classification methods are prediction models and algorithms used to classify or categorize objects based on their measurements. Analysis typically begins with "labeled" data, which are observations with measurements on one or more explanatory variables (features) as well as a label variable (outcome) indicating which class the object belongs to. The goal is to construct a classification rule from these labeled observations that can predict the class of future, unlabeled observations that share the same explanatory variables.

In this dissertation, this goal translates into the building of a classification rule for \textbf{Employment\_Status (Employed vs. unemployed)} using the features of each graduate, such as GPA, Internship\_Experience, and Education\_Level.


\subsubsection{K-Nearest Neighbor (KNN)}

K-Nearest Neighbors is a nonparametric classification method. For each point, we look at its k nearest labeled points and predict its class to be that of the majority of its neighbors, we use the observations in the training set closest to a given point. This requires four steps: deciding the value of k, defining closeness via a distance metric, sorting distances to determine the nearest neighbors; and assigning the class held by the majority of those neighbors. (Hastie et al., 2009, p. 14)

Several distance metrics exist, including the Euclidean, Manhattan, and Mahalanobis distance. This analysis uses the standard choice, Euclidean distance, defined as the square root of the sum of squared differences between two points across all predictors. For $p$-dimensional continuous variables, the distance between two points $x$ and $x_i$ is defined as (Hastie et al., 2009, p.465):
\vspace{-2pt}
\begin{equation}
d(x, x_i) = \sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{p} (x_j - x_{ij})^2}
\end{equation}

Having defined distance, the value of k must then be chosen, and this choice creates a bias-variance trade-off. When k is small, each prediction depends on only nearby points, $k=1$ relies on a single neighbor. This makes the classifier very flexible, a single mislabeled point can flip a prediction. This instability is high variance, and the classifier is said to overfit. When k is large, predictions are averaged over many neighbors instead. This makes predictions stable, so variance is low. The classifier becomes too simple to capture the true classification boundary. This is high bias, and the classifier is said to be underfit (Boehmke and Greenwell, 2020, Chapter 8; Hastie et al., 2009, p. 37). The aim is therefore to choose a value of k that balances these two effects; this is commonly done via 10-fold cross validation rather than an arbitrary choice. (Hastie et al., 2009, pp. 241, 465)



\subsubsection{Classification Tree}

Classification trees are a non-parametric classification method, also known as partitioning methods, since the feature space (the set of possible values of the explanatory variables) is divided into disjoint, non-overlapping regions.(Yang, 2026, Week 4).

A tree is built from a sequence of features and cut-off point chosen on that feature, a given feature can appear more than once with different cut-off points at different splits. Each split leads either to a further split or to a terminal (leaf) node, where a class is predicted by the majority class of training observations in that leaf. To classify a new observation, it starts at the root node and follows the branches determined by whether it satisfies each split condition until it reaches a leaf (Boehmke and Greenwell, 2020, Chapter 9; Yang, 2026, Week 4).

Choosing the best feature and cut-off point at each split requires a measure of node impurity to optimize. This analysis uses the \textbf{Gini index}, defined as(Hastie et al., 2009, p. 309):

\vspace{-5pt}
\begin{equation}
Gini = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \hat{p}_k(1 - \hat{p}_k)
\end{equation}

where $K$ is the total number of classes and $\hat{p}_k$ is the proportion of training observations in the node belonging to class $k$. A pure node (single class) has a Gini index of $0$, while a node with an even mix of classes has a higher Gini index (impure); the split chosen at each stage is the one producing the greatest reduction in impurity. This approach is greedy where at each step, the best split is chosen based only on the current step, without looking ahead to whether a different choice would lead to a better tree in some future step. (Yang, 2026, week 4)

A fully grown tree tends to overfit, so it is \textbf{pruned} to reduce complexity. Pruning is controlled by a complexity parameter ($cp$), and two strategies were compared to select its value: the \textbf{minimum error rule}, which prunes the tree back to the point of minimum the cross-validated error; and the \textbf{1-SE (smallest tree) rule}, which adds one standard error to the minimum cross-validated error to obtain a threshold, then selects the largest value of ($cp$) (the smallest tree) whose cross-validated error falls within that threshold. The 1-SE rule prunes slightly further than the minimum-error point, typically yielding a smaller, more interpretable tree. (Yang, 2026, week 4; Hastie et al., 2009, p. 313).


\paragraph{Bagging and Random Forest}

Bagging, or bootstrap aggregation, is an ensemble technique that reduces the prediction variance by combining the results of models fitted to different bootstrap samples of the training data. For classification, trees are grown on each sample and the final prediction is made by majority vote. This works particularly well for high-variance, low-bias procedures such as classification trees (Boehmke and Greenwell, 2020, Chapter 10). Random forest is a modification of bagging by restricting each split to a random subset of predictors rather than the full, preventing strong predictors from dominating every tree and producing a more diverse set of trees whose averaged predictions further reduce variance (Hastie et al., 2009, p. 587; Yang, 2026, Week 4).

\subsubsection{Support Vector Machine}


Support Vector Machines (SVM) are a supervised classification technique that finds a boundary separating classes by maximizing the margin. The boundary is a hyperplane, and the margin is the perpendicular distance from the hyperplane to the nearest point(s) of either class. Where multiple hyperplanes could separate the classes, SVM selects the one that maximizes this distance,the largest margin, \textbf{maximal margin hyperplane}. (Hastie et al., 2009, p. 423; Boehmke and Greenwell, 2020, Chapter 14)

Once the optimal hyperplane is found, with estimated weight vector $\hat{w}$ and estimated bias term $\hat{b}$, a new observation $x_{new}$ is classified by $\hat{g}(x_{new}) = \text{sign}(\hat{w}^T x_{new} + \hat{b})$, assigning class $+1$ or $-1$ depending on sign of the result, which side of the hyperplane it falls (Yang, 2026, Week 5).

The boundary described so far is linear. SVM can be extended to nonlinear boundaries by mapping the input features into a higher-dimensional space using a \textbf{kernel function}. Common kernel choices include the \textbf{linear kernel} (no transformation, producing a straight-line boundary), the \textbf{polynomial kernel}, and the \textbf{radial (Gaussian) kernel}, each producing increasingly flexible, curved decision boundaries. The choice of kernel is typically guided by whether the classes appear linearly separable in the original feature space and by comparing predictive performance across kernels. (Boehmke and Greenwell, 2020, Chapter 14; Hastie et al., 2009, p. 424)


\subsection{Model Selection}

Model selection techniques ensure that the analysis retained only the variables with the most interpretable and significant effects. Restricting the model to a smaller subset of predictors can improve overall prediction accuracy and a smaller subset of predictors is also easier to interpret, allowing the most important effects to be identified. (Hastie et al., 2009, p. 57). 

\subsubsection{Stepwise Selection}

Stepwise selection has three types: forward, backward and both-directions (stepwise selection). This analysis used stepwise selection, where variables can be added or removed. At each step, the algorithm considers both removing the variable currently in the model that contributes least (based on AIC/BIC criteria) and adding a variable not currently in the model, continuing until no further step improves the criterion. (Faraway, 2015, p. 151).

\subsubsection{Shrinkage Methods}


Unlike stepwise selection, which searches over discrete subsets of predictions, shrinkage methods fit the full model, but penalize the coefficients. This constraint reduces variance at the cost of a small increase in bias, and depending on the penalty used, we can also perform variable selection by shrinking some coefficient exactly to zero. 

A general way to express this is to minimize the residual sum of squares subject to a combination of two penalty terms (L1 penalty: $\sum_{j=1}^{p}|\beta_j|$ and L2 penalty: $\sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2$)  : 
\vspace{-2pt}
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta} = \arg\min_{\beta} \left\{ \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(y_i - \beta_0 - \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2 + \lambda_1 \sum_{j=1}^{p}|\beta_j| + \lambda_2 \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2 \right\}
\label{eq:general-penalty}
\end{equation}

The tuning parameters $\lambda_1$ and $\lambda_2$ control how much weight is given to each penalty.

\paragraph{LASSO}


LASSO is a penalized regression method. Instead of testing subsets of variables like stepwise selection, it fits all the predictors at once but applies the L1 penalty, which shrinks the coefficients to exactly zero, so it automatically performs variable selection technique. (James et al., 2013, p. 219). Setting $\lambda_2 = 0$ in Equation~\ref{eq:general-penalty} leaves only the L1 penalty.

\paragraph{Ridge}

Ridge regression applies the L2 penalty. Unlike LASSO, it does not shrink coefficients exactly to zero, and therefore does not perform variable selection (James et al., 2013, p. 215). Setting $\lambda_1 = 0$ in Equation~\ref{eq:general-penalty} leaves only the L2 penalty.


\paragraph{Elastic Net}

The elastic net combines both the L1 and L2 penalties, allowing it to perform coefficient shrinkage and variable selection as in Equation~\ref{eq:general-penalty} (Hastie et al., 2009, p. 73).

\subsubsection{Likelihood-Ratio Test}

A likelihood-ratio test compares two nested models, one with fewer predictors than the other, to assess whether the additional predictors in the larger model significantly improve model fit. Under the null hypothesis, the restricted model is adequate, a significant result ($p<0.05$) indicates the larger model fits significantly better than the smaller one. In R, this is implemented via the \texttt{anova()} function. (James et al., 2013, p.116)


\subsection{Statistical Models}

\subsubsection{Multiple Linear Regression}

Multiple linear regression models a continuous response variable as a linear combination of predictors. The equation: $y_i = \beta_0 + \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j x_{ij} + \epsilon_i$ where $y_i$ is the response variable, $x_{ij}$ are the predictor variables, $\beta_j$ are the coefficients to be estimated and $\epsilon_i$ is the error term, assumed to be independently and normally distributed with mean zero and constant variance. (James et al., 2013, p. 71)

\subsubsection{Logistic Regression}

For employment status, a binary outcome (employed/unemployed), predictions must be constrained to lie between 0 and 1. Logistic regression models the probability of the outcome using the logit link function:
\vspace{-2pt}
\begin{equation}
\log\left(\frac{p_i}{1-p_i}\right) = \beta_0 + \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j x_{ij}
\end{equation}

where $p_i = P(y_i = 1 \mid x_i)$ is the probability that individual $i$ is employed, estimated by maximum likelihood (James et al., 2013, p. 132). 

\subsubsection{Generalized Additive Models (GAM)}
Generalized additive models (GAMs) allows continuous predictors to enter the model through smooth, non-linear functions while keeping the model additive and interpretable:

\begin{equation}
y_i = \beta_0 + \sum_{k} f_k(x_{ik}) + \sum_{j} \beta_j x_{ij} + \epsilon_i
\end{equation}

where $f_k(\cdot)$ are smooth functions (typically splines) fitted to continuous predictors  and $\beta_j$ are linear coefficients for categorical predictors (Hastie et al., 2009, p. 297).


\subsection{Model Evaluation Metrics}

After applying a classification method, each observation is assigned a predicted class, which may match or differ from its true class. 

\subsubsection{Confusion Matrix}

It is a cross-tabulation of the predicted class versus the true class, where the$ij$-th element in the table gives the number of observations that were actually in class $i$ but predicted to be in class $j$. 

Assume we have two classes: sick ($y=1$) as the True class, and healthy($y=0$) as the False class. We want to predict whether a person is sick or healthy. A positive prediction means the person is predicted to be sick , and a negative prediction means the person is predicted to be healthy (Yang, 2026, Week 3). These four cases fill the confusion matrix as follows Table~\ref{tbl:confusionMatrix} (Boehmke and Greenwell, 2020, Chapter 2):

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{True Positive (TP):} a person is sick, correctly predicted to be sick
\item \textbf{False Positive (FP):} a person is healthy, incorrectly predicted to be sick
\item \textbf{True Negative (TN):} a person is healthy, correctly predicted to be healthy
\item \textbf{False Negative (FN):} a person is sick, incorrectly predicted to be healthy

\end{itemize}

