\vspace{-2pt}
From these values, we can find the following performance metrics:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Sensitivity/Recall (True Positive Rate)}: the proportion of all observations that we correctly predicted as \textbf{positive}, $TP/(TP+FN)$.
\item \textbf{Specificity (True Negative Rate)}: the proportion of all observations that were correctly predicted as \textbf{negative}, $TN/(TN+FP)$.
\item \textbf{Accuracy (Correct Classification Rate)}: the number of correctly classified observations in their true class, whether positive or negative; this lies on the diagonal of the table, $(TP+TN)/n$. 
\textit{(Misclassification Rate is $1-\text{accuracy}$)} (James et al., 2013, p.158)
\item \textbf{Precision (Positive Predictive Value)}: the proportion of correctly predicted positive observations, $TP/(TP+FP)$.
\item \textbf{$F_1$ Score}: a single-number summary of precision and recall, $F_1 = 2 \times \frac{\text{Precision} \times \text{Recall}}{\text{Precision} + \text{Recall}}$ 
\end{itemize} 

\subsubsection{Precision-Recall and ROC Curves}

\begin{itemize}


\item \textbf{Precision-Recall Curve}: used when the class distribution is imbalanced. It plots recall on the x-axis against precision on the y-axis, capturing how many of the actual positives were correctly caught and capturing how many of the predicted positives were actually correct, focusing specifically on performance of the \textbf{positive class only}. Overall performance is summarized by the area under the curve (AUC), with values closer to 1 indicating stronger precision-recall trade-off (Davis, Goadrich, 2006, p.2).

\item \textbf{ROC (Receiver Operating Characteristic)}: used to assess the trade-off between sensitivity and specificity for a binary classifier. It plots sensitivity on the y-axis against $1 - \text{Specificity}$ on the x-axis, capturing both how many actual positives are correctly caught and how many actual negatives are incorrectly classified as positive. Overall performance is summarized by the area under the curve (AUC), with values closer to 1 indicating better discrimination between classes (Davis, Goadrich, 2006, p.2) 


\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{RMSE and $R^2$}

Root mean squared error (RMSE) and the coefficient of determination ($R^2$) are used to evaluate the predictive performance of regression models. RMSE measures how far, on average, the model's predictions are from the actual value, with lower values indicating better predictive performance. $R^2$ represents the proportion of variance in the response variable explained by the model, ranging from 0 to 1, with value closer to 1 indicating a better fit (Boehmke and Greenwell, 2020, Chapter 2).



