\section{Analysis of the data}

\subsection{Research Question 1: Classification models to predict employment status}
\label{sec:RS1}

For this question, \texttt{Employment\_Status} was filtered to the Employed/Unemployed categories to frame this as a binary classification problem. Non-informative columns were removed: \texttt{Region\_of\_Study} (constant at "UK" after filtering), \texttt{Salary} and \texttt{Job\_Sector}, since these are outcomes of employment status rather than predictors of it.

\subsubsection{K-Nearest Neighbors Method}

\texttt{Employment\_Status} was set as the response variable (Y), with the remaining variables forming the predictor set (X). The categorical predictors in X were converted into dummy variables, while the continuous predictors were identified separately so that they could be scaled later. A seed was fixed at 1 throughout to ensure reproducible results.


The data set was then divided into 80\% training sets and 20\% test sets. Since KNN relies on distance calculations between observations, unscaled variables with larger ranges would dominate the distance metric, so the continuous predictors were standardized. To avoid data leakage, the mean and standard deviation were computed using only the training data, and then were applied to scale the continuous predictors in both the training and test sets.

\vspace{-10pt}
\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{Rplot12.png}
    \caption{10-Fold Cross-Validation Accuracy vs k}
    \label{fig:knn-cv}
\end{figure}
\vspace{-5pt}
The KNN models were fitted using the \texttt{knn()} function from the \texttt{class} package in R (James et al., 2013, p. 163), testing fifty candidate values of $k$ (1 to 50) using a 10-fold cross-validation, as described in Section~\ref{sec:methods}. For each candidate value of $k$, the training data was split into 10 folds, each fold once as validation while the remaining nine were used for training(Hastie et al., 2009, pp. 242). This produced 10 accuracy estimates per value of $k$, which were averaged to give a cross-validated accuracy for that $k$. Repeating this process in all fifty candidate values of $k$ produced the cross-validated accuracy curve shown in Figure~\ref{fig:knn-cv}. The value of $k$ that maximized cross-validated precision, $k=28$, was selected as the final model, which means that each prediction is based on the $28$ closest neighboring observations.

Using this value of $k$, class predictions were made on the test 
set and test accuracy was computed as the proportion of correctly 
classified observations i.e. $\text{mean}(y_{\text{test}} = \hat{y})$. 

\subsubsection{Classification Trees}

The data was split again into 80\% training set and 20\% test sets, using the same seed for consistency between methods. Employment\_Status was set as a factor to indicate a classification, rather than a regression task.

A full classification tree was fitted using the \texttt{rpart()} function from the \texttt{rpart} package (Boehmke and Greenwell, 2020, hapter 9) with cost complexity $cp=0$, letting it be repeatedly split until every terminal node was pure. This produced a large, overfit tree. The tree was therefore pruned, using the approaches described
in Section~\ref{sec:methods}: the minimum cross-validated error rule and the 1-SE rule.

Pruning via the minimum-error rule selected $cp=0.0004$, producing a tree with $21$ splits. The 1-SE rule with $19$ splits selected a smaller and less complex tree. A 2-SE variant was also considered, using two standard errors instead of one; this produced a similarly sized tree (18 splits) with negligible difference in predictive performance, so the more standard, commonly used 1-SE tree was retained.

To decide between pruning strategies, and to check whether a single tree was competitive with ensemble methods, four models were compared on test accuracy: the minimum-error tree, the 1-SE tree, bagging, and random forest (fitted using the \texttt{randomForest()} function from the \texttt{randomForest} package (James et al., 2013, p. 329)), both grown on bootstrap samples of the training data, as described in Section~\ref{sec:methods}. Table~\ref{tbl:tree-comparison} shows the minimum-error tree achieved the highest accuracy at 0.9073 and the 1-SE tree close behind at 0.9069.

Despite not achieving the highest accuracy, the 1-SE tree was selected as the model to report in detail, as it offers a substantially simpler partition of the feature space.


\subsubsection{Support Vector Machine (SVM)}

As a further approach, we fitted support vector machine (SVM) models, using the \texttt{svm()} function from the \texttt{e1071} (James et al., 2013, p. 359), with four different kernels: linear, radial(gaussian), polynomial of degree 2 and polynomial of degree 3. A seed of 1 was set. This was by far the most computationally expensive step in the analysis: tuning the linear kernel took over two hours to run. Given this cost, we focused on trying different kernels rather than tuning. Then each of the four fitted models was used to predict employment status on the same test set used for the classification tree.

Table~\ref{tbl:SVM} shows that the polynomial of degree 2 kernel achieved the highest accuracy among the four models. This kernel introduces a quadratic decision boundary, allowing for moderate non-linearity while remaining less flexible than the radial kernel and less prone to overfitting than the higher degree polynomial kernel.

