\subsection{Research Question 3: Which factors are the most important predictors of employment outcomes?}
\label{sec:RS3}


In this section, we investigate the factors are the most important predictors of employment status. To answer this, we identify the best-fitting model using two approaches explained in Section~\ref{sec:methods}: stepwise subset selection and Lasso regression (Hastie et al., 2009, p. 69).  

Since employment status is a binary response, we frame this as a logistic regression problem, where the goal is to estimate the probability of being employed versus unemployed. We first constructed a binary response variable, coding "Employed" as 1 and "Unemployed" as 0, and removed the original Employment\_Status variable now that Employment\_binary serves as the response variable. A seed set to 1 to ensure reproducibility of results. Since we are also interested in the predictive accuracy of the resulting model, we also split the data into training and test data sets. 


We first fit a full logistic model containing all predictors using the \texttt{glm()} function from the \texttt{stats} package in R and applied stepwise selection with AIC criterion using \texttt{stepAIC()} with default AIC penalty $k=2$. This flagged \textbf{Gender} as a non-significant predictor, with p-values above 0.05 for both the Male and Other categories. To confirm this, we compared the AIC-selected model against a nested model with \texttt{Gender} removed using a likelihood-ratio test (\texttt{anova()}). The nested model was selected and we generated the predictions on the test data and calculated the classification accuracy. As a second stepwise approach, we repeated this procedure using \texttt{stepAIC()} with the BIC criterion (setting $k = \log(n)$, where n is the number of observations), again generating predictions and accuracy on the test data. 

The resulting accuracies were 0.8533724 for the AIC model after removing Gender and 0.8533724 for the BIC model, which are exactly the same.\\

The second approach considered was Lasso regression,  which, as discussed earlier in Section~\ref{sec:methods}, automatically performs variable selection by shrinking the coefficients of non-informative predictors to exactly zero. To fit the LASSO model, the data set was split into training and test data set of the predictor matrix `X` and the response vector `Y` (\texttt{Employment\_binary}). For comparison, we also fitted ridge regression ($\alpha=0$) and elastic net ($\alpha=0.5$) using the same procedure applied to LASSO, though only LASSO results are discussed in detail in this report. Figure~\ref{fig:lasso} shows the coefficient path of the LASSO model fitted with \texttt{glmnet()}, using a binary family and $\alpha=1$ (Hastie et al., 2026).
