In this plot, coefficient values are shown on the y-axis and $-\log(\lambda)$ is shown on the bottom x-axis. As $-\log(\lambda)$ increases from left to right, $\lambda$ itself decreases, so the penalty on the coefficients becomes weaker. The top axis shows the number of non-zero coefficients in the model at each point: at the far left (strong penalty) with only 2 predictors remaining non-zero; at far right (low penalty), 25 predictors remain non-zero. Each colored line represents one predictor, or, for a categorical variable, one dummy-coded level. 

The direction of each line indicates the sign of that predictor's coefficient on the log-odds of being employed. The numbers on the right edge of the plot correspond to the column indices of \textbf{X\_train}. For example, tracing index 28 shows that this is \texttt{GPA}. This line is moving upward (positive coefficient), meaning that a higher GPA is associated with higher probability of being employed.

This plot illustrates how the number of non-zero coefficients changes with respect to the values of $\lambda$, but it does not identify the best-performing model. For that, we use cross-validation via \texttt{cv.glmnet()} to find the best $\lambda$ (Hastie et al., 2026; James et al., 2013, p. 227).


After applying \texttt{cv.glmnet()}, we selected the cross-validated lambda value that minimizes deviance, \texttt{lambda.min} equal to 0.0008, and refit the model using \texttt{glmnet()} with this value. We then evaluated the model's performance in predicting employment status on the test set. The same cross-validation procedure was applied to select $\lambda$ for the ridge and elastic net models. Table~\ref{tbl:result-shrinkagemethods} shows that LASSO achieved the highest accuracy at 0.8519 among the three shrinkage methods.


Using LASSO as a variable-selection method, we examined the coefficients of the LASSO model to identify which predictors were shrunk to exactly zero. The following variables were reduced to exactly zero: Age, all three categories of \texttt{Visa\_Type}, only two of the eight categories of \texttt{Country\_of\_Origin} and one of the six categories of \texttt{Field\_of\_Study}. We conclude it was reasonable to drop \texttt{Age} and \texttt{Visa\_Type}.


We then refit a GLM using all predictors except \texttt{Age} and \texttt{Visa\_Type}, in which \texttt{Gender} had a p-value above 0.05, indicating non-significance; so we removed it and confirmed the nested model  (without \texttt{Gender}) was preferred using \texttt{anova()}. We repeated this process over several steps, sequentially removing \texttt{Field\_of\_Study} and then \texttt{Country\_of\_Origin}, arriving at a final model containing \texttt{Education\_Level}, \texttt{GPA}, \texttt{Language\_Proficiency}, \texttt{University\_Ranking},  \texttt{Years\_Since\_Graduation}, and \texttt{Internship\_Experience}.

A notable finding was that this final LASSO-informed model, the BIC-selected model, and the AIC-selected model (after removing Gender) all converged on the same predictors, coefficients, p-values, and test-set accuracy of \textbf{0.8533724}.

We next explored whether meaningful interaction effects existed among predictors, motivated by sensible reasoning. A higher GPA may increase the likelihood of securing an internship, motivating an interaction between \texttt{GPA} and \texttt{Internship\_Experience}. The likelihood of securing an internship may also depend on university ranking, as students from higher-ranked universities may be perceived by employers as more capable or better prepared, and education level may moderate the effect of language proficiency on employment outcomes.



