As a second approach, we fitted a generalized additive model (GAM) using the \texttt{mgcv} package with \texttt{method = "REML"}, restricted to the variables retained by the BIC-selected linear model.

After fitting the initial additive model, we examined the smooth function plots to check for non-linear relationships between the continuous predictors (\texttt{Age}, \texttt{Years\_Since\_Graduation}, \texttt{GPA}) and \texttt{Salary}. \texttt{GPA} was the only predictor showing a clearly non-linear effect on \texttt{Salary}.

We then examined the model summary and removed non-significant predictors one at a time, confirming each removal with a likelihood-ratio test (\texttt{anova()}) between the reduced and full models. Following this procedure, we sequentially removed \texttt{Gender}, \texttt{Visa\_Type}, \texttt{Field\_of\_Study}, \texttt{Country\_of\_Origin}, and finally the linear continuous terms:  \texttt{Age} and \texttt{Years\_Since\_Graduation}. Finishing with a final model containing \texttt{GPA}, , \texttt{Education\_Level}, \texttt{
Internship\_Experience}, \texttt{University\_Ranking}, and \texttt{Language\_Proficiency}.

For this step-by-step comparison, each candidate model was refitted using \texttt{method = "ML"} rather than \texttt{"REML"}, since \texttt{REML} likelihoods are not valid for comparing models with different predictors, unlike \texttt{ML}. Once the final variable set was settled, the model was refit with \texttt{method = "REML"}.


The plot below shows the non linear effect of \texttt{GPA} with \texttt{Salary} (Figure~\ref{fig:salaryGPA}). The estimated smooth term for GPA has an effective degrees of freedom (edf) of approximately 8.70. This is far from the edf$= 1$ that would indicate a linear relationship, confirming a genuinely non-linear, fairly complex relationship between GPA and salary. 

The direction of this relationship is unexpected and non-monotonic: predicted salary is highest at lowest GPA values, drops sharply to minimum around the middle of the range (GPA between 2.5-3.0), then partially recovers before dropping again at the highest GPA values. One plausible, non-statistical explanation is that GPA and career-relevant employability skills are not the same thing. Students who prioritize maximizing their GPA may spend relatively more time on coursework and less time on internships, networking, or other activities employers value. This could explain the initial decline, though not the partial recovers at higher GPA. \\

We also examined whether allowing for interaction effects changed the model. Using the same interaction terms considered in section ~\ref{sec:RS3} (GPA:Internship\_Experience, Internship\_Experience: University\_Ranking, and Education\_Level:Language\_Proficiency), a likelihood-ratio test indicated that the interaction model provided a significantly better fit than the additive model. Fitting this interaction model, however, changed the shape of the estimated GPA effect quite substantially, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:GPA}.


