\section{Conclusions and discussion}


This dissertation examined what shapes employment outcomes for international graduates in the UK using classification and regression models. The findings suggest that employability depends less on academic performance alone and more on factors employers can directly observe: language proficiency, institutional reputation, and practical work experience. \texttt{GPA} played a more complex role.


This is evident in the classification results, where the first split in the best-performing pruned tree was on \texttt{Language\_Proficiency}, identifying it as the strongest predictor of employment status. Stepwise selection and LASSO converged on the same six predictors (\texttt{Education\_Level}, \texttt{Language\_Proficiency}, \texttt{University\_Ranking}, \texttt{GPA}, \\\texttt{Years\_Since\_Graduation}, and \texttt{Internship\_Experience}), with an interaction model improving fit. This convergence across methods strengthens confidence that these predictors are genuinely important. 



The salary results complicate this as \texttt{GPA} on its own showed a non-linear, complex relationship with salary, resolved once modeled as an interaction with \texttt{Internship\_Experience}. The two do not act independently but interact, suggesting that academic performance alone may be a weak signal of employability. Similarly, \texttt{Years\_Since\_Graduation} was associated with employment status but not salary. The dataset itself cannot explain this directly, though one hypothesis is that it may reflects accumulated job search experience or labor market familiarity, a signal relevant to finding employment but not how well it pays.

Overall, these findings suggest that no single factor determines employability.



\subsection{Limitations}
\texttt{Employment\_Status} was imbalanced between classes, so even the best-performing tree still misses a meaningful proportion of truly employed graduates. The dataset was dominated by categorical variables, with few continuous predictors, which may have limited how well relationships such as salary variation could be captured and modeled.

\subsection{Recommendations}
These findings align closely with those of an employability research internship at the College of Science and Engineering, University of Glasgow, in which I personally participated, examining the gap between students' and employers' expectations about employability. It's qualitative findings and this dissertation's quantitative analysis reinforce one another; both point to the importance of internships. A practical solution is embedding internship within curriculum rather than treating them as optional, and providing careers support tailored to each school or discipline. Together, they give universities a strong evidence base for prioritizing this support.

